Locality-Controlled Out-of-Distribution Guidance for Robust Regulatory DNA Sequence Design Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 arXiv:2610.41736v1 [cs.LG] 30 Oct 2026 Locality-Controlled Out-of-Distribution Guidance for Robust Regulatory DNA Sequence Design Dabin Jeong Affiliation: Wellcome Sanger Institute Email: dj16@sanger.ac.uk Marwane Bourdim Affiliation: Wellcome Sanger Institute Email: mb57@sanger.ac.uk Mo Lotfollahi Affiliation: Wellcome Sanger Institute Email: ml10@sanger.ac.uk Abstract Designing regulatory DNA sequences for targeted gene-expression control is an important challenge. Recent approaches frame this task as property-conditioned generation, where diffusion models trained on natural sequences are guided toward high predicted activity through reward-based fine-tuning or sampling-time guidance. We show that single-reward evaluation can obscure reward-hacking behavior. By decomposing evaluation into property, novelty, and plausibility, we identify an empirically underexplored region of the evaluation space in which all three objectives are satisfied, but no existing baseline succeeds. We postulate that this gap arises because prior methods treat guidance primarily as a matter of magnitude. In regulatory DNA, however, sequence grammar is spatially nonuniform: motif positions are sensitive to perturbation, while background regions allow more exploration. We therefore propose LocoGen by reformulating guidance as a problem of locality . LocoGen learns an invariant motif mask through sequence-level invariance learning to protect motif positions, while applying OOD damping only to background positions. It operates at sampling time on a frozen diffusion backbone, without retraining the backbone. Across HepG2, K562, and SK-N-SH, LocoGen reaches this previously underexplored region of the property–novelty–plausibility space. Code is available at https://github.com/Lotfollahi-lab/LocoGen . 1 Introduction Diffusion models are powerful generative models used in many domains, including images, language, molecules, and biological sequences. Beyond unconditional generation, recent work increasingly focuses on property-conditioned generation, such as generating regulatory DNA sequences that optimize a target property (e.g. gene expression). These advances make diffusion models useful for de novo regulatory DNA design, where candidate sequences can be generated and tested in a lab-in-the-loop setting. We argue that the generated candidates must satisfy three axes simultaneously: (i) high target property , (ii) novelty , and (iii) plausibility —preservation of the natural grammar of regulatory DNA (e.g. motif usage diversity/density). No single axis suffices. High property without plausibility fails to reproduce in the wet lab; high novelty without plausibility drifts off the functional manifold; high property without novelty merely reproduces the training distribution. Only when all three axes are jointly satisfied is a candidate worth its wet-lab budget. To date, DNA sequence generative models have largely treated candidate generation as a single-objective problem: maximizing the target property. However, when evaluation is decomposed into property, novelty, and plausibility, methods that appear strong under single-axis evaluation reveal trade-offs on the remaining axes (Fig. 1 B). Moreover, their apparent property gains often arise not from targeted regulatory improvement, but from distortions of regulatory motif structure, exposing a common limitation of existing guidance methods (§ 5.3 ). Although these methods differ in their reward-hacking mechanisms, they all control optimization through a single scalar magnitude knob (e.g. guidance scale) without specifying where in the sequence the reward should act. This is problematic because regulatory DNA grammar is positional: motif sites are highly constrained, whereas intervening background regions are more permissive. Thus, in the property–novelty–plausibility design space, current baselines fall short of the desired regime: high activity on both readouts, high novelty, and low compositional deviation. We call this unoccupied region the empty corner , and ask whether guidance can shift the empirical trade-off landscape toward it. We propose LocoGen , a locality-controlled guidance method for de novo regulatory DNA generation (Fig. 1 A). Rather than increasing guidance globally, LocoGen controls where it acts. A masked separator model identifies foreground and background positions with an invariant mask; during sampling, out-of-distribution (OOD) guidance is applied only to background positions. This generates novel sequences in permissive regions while preserving regulatory motifs, thereby improving novelty without sacrificing enhancer-like composition or functional activity. Contributions. • Diagnostic framework. We introduce a three-axis evaluation framework for regulatory DNA generation: property, novelty, and compositional plausibility. It reveals two reward-hacking modes concealed by single-axis evaluation: predictor-specific motif concentration and global compositional drift in previous DNA sequence generative models. • Locality-controlled guidance. We reformulate sampling-time guidance as a locality problem and instantiate it as localized OOD control with an invariant subsequence mask. The method operates on a frozen backbone, requires no retraining, and exposes per-position spatial control absent from standard classifier-guidance methods. Figure 1 : LocoGen balances property, novelty, and plausibility. 2 Related Work Discrete diffusion and flow matching on biological sequences. Two frameworks have emerged for generative modeling of categorical sequences. Discrete diffusion operates directly on categorical sequences and learns to denoise progressively masked tokens ( Austin et al., 2021 ; Sahoo et al., 2024 ; Lou et al., 2024 ) . Continuous diffusion and flow matching instead work in a continuous relaxation of the categorical space, typically the probability simplex whose vertices correspond to the discrete tokens ( Stark et al., 2024 ; Davis et al., 2024 ) Both have been applied to regulatory DNA design ( Stark et al., 2024 ; DaSilva et al., 2026 ; Tang et al., 2025 ) . We build on the discrete diffusion line, using a pretrained masked discrete diffusion language model (MDLM) ( Sahoo et al., 2024 ) as a backbone and steering generation with inference-time guidance. Reward-based fine-tuning of diffusion backbones. A first family of methods directly fine-tunes the backbone against a reward signal, often framed as reinforcement learning. DRAKES ( Wang et al., 2025 ) backpropagates a differentiable reward to pre-trained masked discrete diffusion model; DRaFT ( Clark et al., 2024 ) applies the same differentiable-reward principle in the continuous setting. When the reward is non-differentiable, policy-gradient or gradient-free alternatives are used instead ( Cao et al., 2025 ) . These methods can each optimize toward a particular property, but they do not expose a post-hoc control knob: changing the target reward generally requires re-specifying the training objective and running a separate fine-tuning pass, even when recent variants reduce its cost or handle multiple objectives jointly. Although recent methods reduce the cost of retraining ( Cao et al., 2025 ) , they inherently require backbone retraining for each new objective. Training-free guidance. Classifier guidance instead allows the guidance signal to be adjusted entirely at inference time through an external scoring network ( Uehara et al., 2025 ; Rojas et al., 2026 ) . DNA-Diffusion ( DaSilva et al., 2026 ) , by contrast, conditions on cell-type labels rather than optimizing a reward, and therefore serves as a non-optimizing reference rather than a competing optimizer. Another method that bypasses backbone retraining is by sampling many candidates and selecting the highest-scoring ones. SVDD ( Li et al., 2024 ) performs soft value-based decoding, evaluating generated candidates at each step and resampling proportionally to a learned value. 3 Preliminary 3.1 Notation We consider DNA sequences of fixed length L L over the nucleotide alphabet 𝒜 = { A , T , G , C } \mathcal{A}=\{A,T,G,C\} . A DNA sequence is denoted by X = ( x 1 , … , x L ) ∈ 𝒜 L X=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{L})\in\mathcal{A}^{L} , where x ℓ ∈ 𝒜 x_{\ell}\in\mathcal{A} represents the nucleotide at position ℓ \ell . Its one-hot representation is denoted by 𝐱 ∈ { 0 , 1 } L × 4 \mathbf{x}\in\{0,1\}^{L	imes 4} , where each row 𝐱 ℓ ∈ { 0 , 1 } 4 \mathbf{x}_{\ell}\in\{0,1\}^{4} satisfies ∑ k = 1 4 𝐱 ℓ , k = 1 \sum_{k=1}^{4}\mathbf{x}_{\ell,k}=1 . 3.2 Problem formulation We consider paired observations ( X , Y ) (X,Y) , where X ∈ 𝒳 = 𝒜 L X\in\mathcal{X}=\mathcal{A}^{L} denotes a DNA sequence and Y ∈ 𝒴 ⊆ ℝ Y\in\mathcal{Y}\subseteq\mathbb{R} denotes an associated scalar property (e.g., target gene activity). Let p θ ​ ( X ) p_{	heta}(X) denote a base generative model over DNA sequences, and let p ϕ ​ ( Y ∣ X ) p_{\phi}(Y\mid X) denote an auxiliary property predictor. For a target property value y ⋆ y^{\star} , classifier-based guidance aims to reweight the base generative distribution according to the predicted property likelihood as follows, p guide ​ ( X ∣ y ⋆ ) ∝ p θ ​ ( X ) ​ p ϕ ​ ( y ⋆ ∣ X ) λ , p_{\mathrm{guide}}(X\mid y^{\star})\propto p_{	heta}(X)\,p_{\phi}(y^{\star}\mid X)^{\lambda}, (1) where λ ≥ 0 \lambda\geq 0 controls the guidance strength. Since our goal is to generate novel regulatory sequences, effective generation guidance must balance three objectives: (i) achieving the target property y ⋆ y^{\star} , (ii) producing novel sequences that depart from the training distribution rather than memorizing it, and (iii) maintaining plausibility. 3.3 Masked 