26 ) . All baselines are run with their authors’ released checkpoints and original decoding hyperparameters. We acknowledge concurrent DNA generative models, e.g GLID2E ( Cao et al., 2025 ) , Ctrl-DNA ( Chen et al., 2025 ) , and GFlowNet ( Bengio et al., 2021 ) , but do not include them as baselines since no public checkpoints are available and retraining their upstream models is out of scope. Computation resource requirements are reported in § S1.5 . 5.2 Three-axis evaluation framework We evaluate a generated set 𝒳 ∗ = { x ∗ ( i ) } i = 1 N \mathcal{X}^{*}=\{x^{*(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N} against the training set 𝒳 = { x ( j ) } j = 1 M \mathcal{X}=\{x^{(j)}\}_{j=1}^{M} along three axes, (1) property, (2) novelty, and (3) compositional plausibility under a fixed protocol decoupled from each method’s training or guidance loop. All metrics are reported as mean ± \pm standard deviation across three sampling seeds. 5.2.1 Property axis. We assess functional activity using two complementary held-out predictors from Wang et al. (2025) : regulatory potency, measured by target gene expression, and chromatin accessibility, which is required for transcription factors to bind regulatory sequences and modulate gene expression. See § S2.2 for details. Pred-activity We use an independently released oracle that maps a sequence to a scalar prediction of gene expression, with separate heads for cell lines c ∈ { HepG2 , K562 , SK ​ - ​ N ​ - ​ SH } c\in\{\mathrm{HepG2},\mathrm{K562},\mathrm{SK	ext{-}N	ext{-}SH}\} . For each cell line, we report the median predicted activity over generated samples. ATAC_acc. We use an independently released classifier that maps each sequence to a predicted probability of chromatin accessibility, formulated as a binary classification task distinguishing accessible from inaccessible sequences. We report the mean predicted accessibility over generated samples. 5.2.2 Novelty axis. Authenticity ( Alaa et al., 2022 ) . We report authenticity score to measure novelty 